@using Gleanvolt.Core.Strategies
@implements IDisposable
@inject ITargetedChargeSelector Target
@inject ITargetedChargePreview Preview
@inject IVehicleTelemetry Vehicle
@inject VehicleDisplayOptions VehicleDisplay
@inject IForecastRuntimeSettings ForecastSettings
@inject TargetedDisplayOptions Display
@inject TimeProvider Time

@*
    The old /targeted page, the fourth tab since #98, and since #99 a tab that asks the car before it
    asks the owner.

    Three things happen here, in this order, because that is the order the decision is made in:

      1. What the car says about itself, when there is a feed and a reading. SOC and range are the
         numbers the plan is about to be built from, so they belong above the form rather than a nav
         item away on the dashboard.
      2. What the owner wants, said either way round -- kilowatt-hours to add (the original contract,
         the default, and the only basis an install with no vehicle feed ever sees) or a state of
         charge to reach, which VehicleTargetEnergy converts once, here, at activation.
      3. The plan, before the charger moves. Preview builds it through ITargetedChargePreview, which
         writes to nothing; Start then does what Activate always did -- request first, then the mode,
         because the controller reads both in the same cycle.

    Text, not a chart: the question at 22:00 is "is the car going to be ready, and why is nothing
    happening yet?", and no chart answers that as directly as a sentence.

    Subscribes to the request itself, because it can move without this tab's help -- Home Assistant
    sets one too, and the mode clears itself once the target is met.
*@

<p class="muted">
    Say how much the car needs and when it has to be there, then look at the plan before it starts.
    The plan takes every kilowatt-hour of forecast surplus the home battery has not already claimed,
    and buys the rest from the grid as late as it can and still finish in time.
</p>

@if (_vehicle is not null)
{
    @*
        Only with a feed and a reading. An install with Vehicle:Enabled=false sees the form it has
        always seen, which is the whole "default behaviour is unchanged" promise -- and an empty card
        would be a worse answer than no card.
    *@
    <section class="plan" id="vehicle-card">
        <h2>The car</h2>

        <p class="muted">
            Reported by the car @VehicleAge(_vehicle) ago@(_vehicle.SourceId is null ? "" : $" via {_vehicle.SourceId}").
            @if (_vehicleStale)
            {
                <strong>
                    Stale — older than @(VehicleDisplay.MaxAge.TotalHours.ToString("F0")) h, so the feed may
                    have died. Check the figures against the car before planning from them.
                </strong>
            }
        </p>

        <section class="stat-grid">
            <Stat Label="Car battery" Value="@Percent(_vehicle.SocPercent)"
                  Hint="The car's own state of charge, as its cloud integration last reported it. Not the home battery." />
            <Stat Label="Range" Value="@Km(_vehicle.RangeKm)"
                  Hint="What the car reckons is left in it, off its own recent consumption. Display only — no plan reads it — but it is the figure that answers whether the target you are about to set is enough for the trip." />
            <Stat Label="Plugged in" Value="@PluggedIn()"
                  Hint="What the charger sees at the socket, and what the car says about its own. The charger is the faster of the two and the one the controller acts on; the car can lag it by hours." />
            <Stat Label="Car charge state" Value="@_vehicle.ChargeState.ToString()"
                  Hint="What the car says it is doing: Idle, Charging, Complete, or Unknown when it doesn't report." />
        </section>
    </section>
}

@*
    The basis selector appears only when it can be honoured: a capacity in Vehicle:BatteryCapacityKWh
    and an SOC in the last reading. Without both there is no honest conversion from "80%" to
    kilowatt-hours, and offering a control that would have to guess is worse than not offering it.
*@
@if (CanTargetSoc)
{
    <div class="control">
        <label for="target-basis">What are you aiming at?</label>
        <select id="target-basis" @bind="_basis" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited">
            <option value="@TargetBasis.Energy">Energy to add (kWh)</option>
            <option value="@TargetBasis.Soc">Battery target (%)</option>
        </select>
        <span class="hint">
            Either way the promise is metered in kilowatt-hours at the charger. A battery target is
            converted once, when you start it — a later reading from the car does not move a target
            that is already part delivered.
        </span>
    </div>
}

@if (_basis == TargetBasis.Soc && CanTargetSoc)
{
    <div class="control">
        <label for="target-soc">Charge to (%)</label>
        <input id="target-soc" type="number" step="5" min="0" max="100"
               @bind="_targetSocPercent" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited" />
        <span class="hint">@SocConversionHint()</span>
    </div>
}
else
{
    <div class="control">
        <label for="target-energy">Energy needed (kWh)</label>
        <input id="target-energy" type="number" step="0.5" min="0"
               @bind="_energyKWh" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited" />
        <span class="hint">Measured at the charger, from the moment you press Start.</span>
    </div>
}

@*
    The priority, and the rest SOC under it. Offered on the same terms as the basis selector above:
    the rest point is a state of charge, so without a capacity and a reading there is no honest way to
    know where 80% is, and a control that would have to guess is worse than no control.
*@
@if (CanTargetSoc)
{
    <div class="control">
        <label for="charge-priority">Charging priority</label>
        <select id="charge-priority" @bind="_priority" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited">
            <option value="@TargetedChargePriority.Cheapest">Cheapest — use as much sun as possible</option>
            <option value="@TargetedChargePriority.JustInTime">Just in time — reach the target right before departure</option>
        </select>
        <span class="hint">
            Both meet the departure. <strong>Cheapest</strong> paces the charge across the window and
            takes every watt of sun above that pace, so the target is usually met well before you
            leave. <strong>Just in time</strong> delivers everything up to the rest level the same way,
            then holds the last stretch back so the car is not sitting full overnight — which costs
            grid, because the sun is turned down while it waits.
        </span>
    </div>
}

@if (_priority == TargetedChargePriority.JustInTime && CanTargetSoc)
{
    <div class="control">
        <label for="rest-soc">Rest at (%)</label>
        <input id="rest-soc" type="number" step="5" min="0" max="100"
               @bind="_restSocPercent" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited" />
        <span class="hint">@RestHint()</span>
    </div>
}

<div class="control">
    <label for="target-departure">Ready by</label>
    <input id="target-departure" type="datetime-local"
           @bind="_departure" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnFormEdited" />
    <span class="hint">
        Charging finishes a few minutes before this, so the car is ready rather than still drawing.
        At most @(Display.MaxHorizon.TotalHours.ToString("F0")) hours ahead.
    </span>
</div>

<div class="control">
    <label for="min-battery-soc">Minimum battery SOC (%)</label>
    <input id="min-battery-soc" type="number" step="1" min="0" max="100"
           @bind="_minBatterySocPercent" @bind:event="onchange" @bind:after="OnMinBatterySocSelected" />
    <span class="hint">
        The floor the home battery is not drawn below to feed the car; the grid covers the gap
        instead. The same number the <a href="/charging-plan/forecasted">Forecasted</a> plan works to.
        Doesn't persist across restarts.
    </span>
</div>

<div class="control">
    <button type="button" class="primary" id="preview-target" disabled="@Busy" @onclick="BuildPreview">Preview plan</button>
    <button type="button" class="secondary" id="cancel-target" disabled="@Busy" @onclick="Cancel">Cancel</button>

    @if (_error is not null)
    {
        <p class="error" role="alert">@_error</p>
    }

    <span class="hint">
        Preview writes to nothing — it is the same planner the poll loop runs, asked what would happen
        if you started now. The request does not survive a restart of the service: if it restarts
        overnight the car stops charging, and nothing will have told you.
    </span>
</div>

@*
    The preview and the button that commits it, in that order and inside the same section: what you
    confirm should be the thing you have just read, not a button somewhere above it.
*@
@if (_previewed)
{
    <section class="plan" id="target-preview">
        <h2>The plan, if you start now</h2>

        @if (_previewRequest is { } asked)
        {
            <p class="muted">@Asking(asked)</p>
        }

        @if (_preview is { } preview)
        {
            <TargetedPlanView Plan="preview" />

            @*
                The cost of the choice, said before the button that commits it. A hold is worth making
                with the number in front of you, not discovered on next month's bill.
            *@
            @if (HoldCost() is { } cost)
            {
                <p class="muted" id="hold-cost">@cost</p>
            }
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="muted">
                No poll has completed yet, so there is nothing to build a plan from — no battery SOC,
                no house load, no forecast anchored to an instant. You can still start the target and
                the plan will appear on the first poll.
            </p>
        }

        <div class="control">
            <button type="button" class="primary" id="activate-target" disabled="@Busy" @onclick="Activate">Start charging</button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" id="discard-preview" disabled="@Busy" @onclick="DiscardPreview">Back to the form</button>
        </div>
    </section>
}

@if (Status?.TargetedPlan is { } plan)
{
    <section class="plan">
        <h2>The plan</h2>

        @if (Target.Request is { } running && running.IsSocBased)
        {
            <p class="muted">@Asking(running)</p>
        }

        <TargetedPlanView Plan="plan" />
    </section>
}
else if (Status is null)
{
    <p class="muted">No poll has completed yet.</p>
}
else
{
    <p class="muted">
        The plan is only shown while <strong>Targeted</strong> is the active charge mode.
        Current mode: @Status.Mode.
    </p>
}

@code {
    [Parameter] public ChargeControlStatus? Status { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Busy { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<ChargeControlMode, Task<ChargeActionResult>> Start { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<Task<ChargeActionResult>> Stop { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Which way round the owner is saying what they want. Purely a property of this form.</summary>
    private enum TargetBasis
    {
        Energy,
        Soc,
    }

    private TargetBasis _basis = TargetBasis.Energy;
    private TargetedChargePriority _priority = TargetedChargePriority.Cheapest;
    private double _energyKWh = 20;
    private double _targetSocPercent = 80;
    private double _restSocPercent;
    private DateTime _departure;
    private double _minBatterySocPercent;
    private string? _error;
    private ChargeControlStatus? _seenStatus;
    private VehicleState? _vehicle;
    private bool _vehicleStale;

    // The preview and what it was built for. Held together so Start commits the figures that were on
    // screen, and so any edit can drop both at once -- it must never be possible to confirm a plan
    // that no longer describes the form above it.
    private TargetedChargePlan? _preview;
    private TargetedChargeRequest? _previewRequest;
    private bool _previewed;

    // The same request priced the cheap way, kept only to name what just-in-time costs. Built in the
    // preview and nowhere else: running two plans on every poll to report a counterfactual is not worth
    // the cycles on a Pi, and a running plan should report what it is doing, not what it declined to do.
    private TargetedChargePlan? _cheapestPreview;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Target.Changed += OnRequestChanged;

        _restSocPercent = Display.DefaultRestSocPercent;
        SeedForm(Target.Request);
        _minBatterySocPercent = ForecastSettings.MinBatterySocFloorPercent;
        RefreshVehicle();
    }

    // Only on a genuinely new poll, not on every render of the page above: the floor is a field the
    // owner may be halfway through typing, and a button press elsewhere on the page must not reset it.
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (ReferenceEquals(_seenStatus, Status))
        {
            return;
        }

        _seenStatus = Status;
        _minBatterySocPercent = ForecastSettings.MinBatterySocFloorPercent;

        // The car on the poll's cadence, as the dashboard reads it: vehicle readings arrive minutes
        // apart at best, so this is one subscription rather than two and one render path rather than
        // two. A reading that lands mid-preview does not move the preview -- it was built from the
        // reading that was on screen, and moving it under the owner would be the one thing this whole
        // preview step exists to prevent.
        RefreshVehicle();
    }

    private void OnRequestChanged(TargetedChargeRequest? request)
    {
        SeedForm(request);
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    public void Dispose() => Target.Changed -= OnRequestChanged;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a target may be asked for as a state of charge: a configured pack size and a reported
    /// SOC to measure the gap from. Staleness deliberately does not withdraw it — a parked car's SOC
    /// does not drift, the reading is flagged loudly above, and refusing the basis outright would only
    /// push the owner into converting it in their head from the same stale number.
    /// </summary>
    private bool CanTargetSoc => VehicleDisplay.CanTargetSoc && _vehicle?.SocPercent is not null;

    // The form shows what is actually being worked to, when there is one -- a page opened in a second
    // browser must not offer to overwrite the running request with its own defaults.
    private void SeedForm(TargetedChargeRequest? request)
    {
        if (request is not null)
        {
            _energyKWh = Math.Round(request.RequiredEnergyWh / 1000, 1);
            _departure = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(request.DepartBy, Time.LocalTimeZone).DateTime;

            if (request.TargetSocPercent is { } soc)
            {
                _basis = TargetBasis.Soc;
                _targetSocPercent = soc;
            }

            _priority = request.Priority;

            if (request.RestSocPercent is { } rest)
            {
                _restSocPercent = rest;
            }

            return;
        }

        // Tomorrow morning: the case this mode exists for.
        var localNow = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(Time.GetUtcNow(), Time.LocalTimeZone).DateTime;
        _departure = localNow.Date.AddDays(1).AddHours(7);
    }

    private void RefreshVehicle()
    {
        _vehicle = Vehicle.GetCurrentState();
        _vehicleStale = _vehicle is not null && _vehicle.IsStaleAt(Time.GetUtcNow(), VehicleDisplay.MaxAge);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Any edit drops the preview. The alternative is a plan on screen describing figures that have
    /// since changed, above a button that says start.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnFormEdited() => DiscardPreview();

    private void DiscardPreview()
    {
        _preview = null;
        _previewRequest = null;
        _cheapestPreview = null;
        _previewed = false;
    }

    private void BuildPreview()
    {
        if (!TryCompose(Time.GetUtcNow(), out var request, out var error))
        {
            _error = error;
            DiscardPreview();
            return;
        }

        _error = null;
        _previewRequest = request;

        // Null when no poll has landed yet, which the section above says in words rather than
        // pretending to a plan built on zeroes. Either way the owner gets the Start button: a service
        // that has only just come up can still be given a target.
        _preview = Preview.Preview(request);

        // What the same energy by the same time would have cost without the hold. Only when a hold was
        // actually planned: on a request with nothing above the rest point the two plans are the same
        // plan, and "0.0 kWh more" is a sentence about nothing.
        _cheapestPreview = request.HoldsTail
            ? Preview.Preview(request with { Priority = TargetedChargePriority.Cheapest, TailEnergyWh = 0 })
            : null;

        _previewed = true;
    }

    private async Task Activate()
    {
        // Recomposed rather than reusing the previewed request: activation is metered from the instant
        // it happens, and the owner may have read the plan for a minute before pressing Start. Every
        // figure that shapes the plan is unchanged -- an edit would have dropped the preview.
        if (!TryCompose(Time.GetUtcNow(), out var request, out var error))
        {
            _error = error;
            DiscardPreview();
            return;
        }

        _error = null;

        // The request first, then the mode: the controller reads both in the same cycle, and a mode
        // selected a poll before its request would report "no target set" for that poll.
        Target.Set(request, "Web UI");

        // Which also writes the charger's use-mode Fast. A charger that refuses it leaves the mode
        // where it was, and the request set above with nothing driving it -- so the request is
        // dropped again rather than left looking active. The failure itself is reported by the
        // header, next to the mode that did not move.
        var result = await Start(ChargeControlMode.Targeted);
        if (!result.Succeeded)
        {
            Target.Clear("Web UI");
            return;
        }

        // The running plan takes over from here, and two plans on one tab -- one of them a quote for a
        // charge that has already started -- would be one too many.
        DiscardPreview();
    }

    // The same Off the header carries, plus the one thing it cannot know to do: drop the request, so
    // nothing is left looking like a promise after the button that says stop.
    private async Task Cancel()
    {
        _error = null;
        DiscardPreview();

        Target.Clear("Web UI");
        await Stop();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Turns the form into a request, or explains why it cannot be one. The single place both Preview
    /// and Start go through, so the two can never disagree about what is valid.
    /// </summary>
    private bool TryCompose(DateTimeOffset now, out TargetedChargeRequest request, out string? error)
    {
        request = default!;
        var departBy = Compose(_departure);

        double energyWh;
        double? targetSoc = null;
        double? socNow = null;

        if (_basis == TargetBasis.Soc && CanTargetSoc)
        {
            socNow = _vehicle!.SocPercent;
            var requiredWh = VehicleTargetEnergy.RequiredWh(
                socNow, _targetSocPercent, VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency);

            if (requiredWh is not { } wh || wh <= 0)
            {
                error = $"The car is already at {Percent(socNow)}, at or above the {_targetSocPercent:F0}% asked for.";
                return false;
            }

            energyWh = wh;
            targetSoc = _targetSocPercent;
        }
        else
        {
            if (_energyKWh <= 0)
            {
                error = "Enter how much energy the car needs.";
                return false;
            }

            energyWh = _energyKWh * 1000;
        }

        if (departBy <= now)
        {
            error = "The departure time is in the past.";
            return false;
        }

        if (departBy - now > Display.MaxHorizon)
        {
            error = $"A departure more than {Display.MaxHorizon.TotalHours:F0} hours away is further than "
                + "the forecast — or a request that does not survive a restart — can honestly promise.";
            return false;
        }

        request = new TargetedChargeRequest(energyWh, departBy, now, targetSoc, socNow)
            with
            {
                Priority = _priority,
            };

        // The split, made once and here — the same place and the same moment as the SOC → kWh
        // conversion above, so a reading that lands later cannot move a promise already part delivered.
        // The planner is handed watt-hours and never sees a percentage again.
        if (_priority == TargetedChargePriority.JustInTime && CanTargetSoc)
        {
            var endSoc = targetSoc ?? VehicleTargetEnergy.ResultingSocPercent(
                _vehicle?.SocPercent, energyWh, VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency);

            var tailWh = endSoc is { } end
                ? VehicleTargetEnergy.TailAboveRestWh(
                    _vehicle?.SocPercent, end, _restSocPercent,
                    VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency)
                : null;

            request = request with
            {
                // Never more than the request itself: a rest point below where the car already sits
                // would otherwise hold back energy the request never contained.
                TailEnergyWh = Math.Clamp(tailWh ?? 0, 0, energyWh),
                RestSocPercent = _restSocPercent,
            };
        }

        error = null;
        return true;
    }

    /// <summary>The request in the terms it was asked in, which for a SOC target is not kilowatt-hours.</summary>
    private static string Asking(TargetedChargeRequest request) =>
        request.TargetSocPercent is { } target
            ? $"Charging to {target:F0}%, from the {request.VehicleSocPercentAtRequest:F0}% the car last "
                + $"reported: {request.RequiredEnergyWh / 1000:F1} kWh at the charger, charge losses included."
            : $"{request.RequiredEnergyWh / 1000:F1} kWh at the charger.";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the hold will actually amount to, in the terms the owner set it in. The interesting case is
    /// a car already past the rest point: there is no tail to hold, the whole charge is the tail, and
    /// saying so is better than silently behaving like Cheapest.
    /// </summary>
    private string RestHint()
    {
        if (_vehicle?.SocPercent is not { } socNow)
        {
            return "No usable SOC reading to measure the rest point from.";
        }

        if (socNow >= _restSocPercent)
        {
            return $"The car is already at {Percent(socNow)}, at or above the {_restSocPercent:F0}% rest level, so "
                + "the whole charge is the held stretch and none of it will start until the release time.";
        }

        var endSoc = _basis == TargetBasis.Soc && CanTargetSoc
            ? _targetSocPercent
            : VehicleTargetEnergy.ResultingSocPercent(
                socNow, _energyKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency) ?? 0;

        if (endSoc <= _restSocPercent)
        {
            return $"This target finishes at about {endSoc:F0}%, at or below the {_restSocPercent:F0}% rest level, so "
                + "there is nothing above it to hold and the charge runs as it would under Cheapest.";
        }

        var tailWh = VehicleTargetEnergy.TailAboveRestWh(
            socNow, endSoc, _restSocPercent, VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency);

        return $"The car charges on the sun up to {_restSocPercent:F0}% whenever the sun is there, then waits. "
            + $"The last {(tailWh ?? 0) / 1000:F1} kWh — about {_restSocPercent:F0}% to {endSoc:F0}% — is scheduled "
            + "to land just before you leave.";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What choosing the hold costs, against the same energy by the same time charged as cheaply as
    /// possible. Empty when there is nothing to say: no comparison built, or the two plans buy the same.
    /// </summary>
    private string? HoldCost()
    {
        if (_preview is not { } held || _cheapestPreview is not { } cheap)
        {
            return null;
        }

        var extraWh = held.GridEnergyWh - cheap.GridEnergyWh;
        if (extraWh <= 100)
        {
            return "Holding the last stretch costs nothing here — the forecast has no sun to give it up.";
        }

        return $"Just in time buys about {extraWh / 1000:F1} kWh more from the grid than charging as cheaply as "
            + $"possible would, because the sun is turned down while the tail waits. That is the trade: the car "
            + $"sits at {_restSocPercent:F0}% overnight instead of at its target.";
    }

    private string SocConversionHint()
    {
        var requiredWh = VehicleTargetEnergy.RequiredWh(
            _vehicle?.SocPercent, _targetSocPercent, VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh * 1000, VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency);

        return requiredWh switch
        {
            null => "No usable SOC reading to measure the gap from.",
            <= 0 => $"The car is already at {Percent(_vehicle?.SocPercent)} — nothing to do.",
            _ => $"From {Percent(_vehicle?.SocPercent)} now, that is about {requiredWh.Value / 1000:F1} kWh at the "
                + $"charger: {VehicleDisplay.BatteryCapacityKWh:F0} kWh usable, {VehicleDisplay.ChargeEfficiency:P0} "
                + "of what the charger meters reaching the cells.",
        };
    }

    // Composed through the app's own zone rather than DateTime.Now, so "07:00" means seven o'clock
    // here whatever the server or the browser thinks, and a DST boundary between now and then cannot
    // move it by an hour.
    private DateTimeOffset Compose(DateTime local) =>
        new(DateTime.SpecifyKind(local, DateTimeKind.Unspecified), Time.LocalTimeZone.GetUtcOffset(local));

    private void OnMinBatterySocSelected() =>
        ForecastSettings.SetMinBatterySocFloorPercent(_minBatterySocPercent, "Web UI");

    private string PluggedIn()
    {
        // Three answers at the socket, not two: before the first poll the charger has not been read at
        // all, and reporting that as "no" would be a claim about hardware nobody has asked yet.
        var charger = Status is null
            ? "Not polled yet"
            : Status.CarConnected ? "Yes at the charger" : "No at the charger";
        var car = _vehicle?.PlugState switch
        {
            VehiclePlugState.Connected => "connected",
            VehiclePlugState.Disconnected => "disconnected",
            _ => "not reported",
        };

        return $"{charger}, {car} by the car";
    }

    // Coarse on purpose, exactly as the dashboard renders it: this data is hours old routinely, so
    // minutes of precision would imply an accuracy the source doesn't have.
    private string VehicleAge(VehicleState vehicle)
    {
        var age = vehicle.AgeAt(Time.GetUtcNow());

        return age switch
        {
            _ when age < TimeSpan.Zero => "just now",
            _ when age < TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1) => "under a minute",
            _ when age < TimeSpan.FromHours(1) => $"{age.TotalMinutes:F0} min",
            _ when age < TimeSpan.FromDays(1) => $"{age.TotalHours:F1} h",
            _ => $"{age.TotalDays:F0} d",
        };
    }

    private static string Percent(double? percent) => percent is { } value ? $"{value:F0}%" : "—";

    private static string Km(double? km) => km is { } value ? $"{value:F0} km" : "—";
}
